# TypeScript Safety

- Prefer explicit types, narrow unions, type guards, and `unknown` over `any`.
- Do not add `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error`, `@ts-nocheck`, or broad `eslint-disable` comments just to silence errors.
- Do not use file-level suppression as a shortcut to make builds or CI pass.
- Keep runtime and type-layer behavior aligned; fix the actual type mismatch instead of hiding it.
- When a value shape is uncertain, model it with a type or runtime guard before accessing properties.

## Preferred fixes

```ts
// Bad
const email = (payload as any).email;

// Good
type RegistrationPayload = { email?: string };
const email = typeof (payload as RegistrationPayload).email === 'string'
  ? (payload as RegistrationPayload).email
  : '';
```

```ts
// Bad
// @ts-ignore
send(data.user.email);

// Good
if (data && typeof data === 'object' && 'user' in data) {
  // narrow further before use
}
```

## Narrow exception

- If suppression is truly unavoidable, keep it to the smallest possible scope, add a short reason, and treat it as temporary technical debt.
- Prefer a single-line, documented exception over a file-wide suppression.
